(defmodule lsp
  "A minimal **Language Server Protocol client** — the first consumer of Brood's
persistent-subprocess primitive (`proc-spawn`/`proc-send`, ADR-104) and a completion
*source* for the multi-source `complete` framework.

A language server is a long-lived child spoken to in JSON-RPC framed with
`Content-Length` headers over stdio. `lsp-connect` spawns one inside a dedicated
**connection process** that owns the `proc` handle (so the server's stdout never
pollutes the editor's mailbox), runs the `initialize` handshake, and then answers
completion requests. `lsp-complete-source` plugs into `*completion-sources*`: on Tab
it does **one bounded synchronous round-trip** to the connection (completion is an
explicit trigger, never a per-keystroke auto-popup, so a short blocking request is
fine), and the returned candidates are merged + fuzzy-ranked with the mode and
buffer-word sources by `complete` like any other source. Narrowing the popup filters that
answer rather than asking again, unless the server said it was incomplete.

The bug-prone logic — frame encode/decode across chunk boundaries, request/response
correlation, completion-result shapes — is **pure** (`lsp-ingest`,
`lsp-issue`, `lsp-decode`, `lsp-completion-entries`, `lsp-completion-candidate`), so it's unit-tested
without a server (tests/lsp_test.blsp); the connection process is a thin IO shell
around it.

No server runs until the editor starts one (`lsp-commands/ed-lsp-start`, or the idle beat's
autostart) and files it on the model under `:lsp-servers`, keyed by its connection
([[lsp-server-entries]]); starting one registers [[lsp-complete-source]] with completion.

Framing is std's `jsonrpc`, which is **byte-accurate**: `Content-Length` is LSP's byte
count, and bodies are cut by UTF-8 byte offset, so multi-byte content — which
rust-analyzer's completion items routinely carry — round-trips correctly. (A multi-byte
character split across two stdout reads is reassembled by the subprocess reader before it
reaches us, so a large frame no longer desyncs.)

Caveats: document positions use 0-based **character** columns, which equal LSP's UTF-16
code units for ASCII. Document sync is INCREMENTAL when the server offers it
(`textDocumentSync` change kind 2): the editor sends the splices its buffer recorded
([[lsp-content-changes]]), not the document — see [[lsp-sync-document]]."
  (:use model) ; ed-current-buf — the source reads the current buffer
  (:use editor/buffer) ; buffer-file / buffer-text / buffer-point / line+column
  (:use editor/highlight) ; symbol-prefix-at — the shared completion word boundary
  (:use lsp-requests)) ; the request-kind records + the LspRequest ability (wire + shape impls below)
;; ---- tunables (redefine live) -----------------------------------------------

(def *lsp-timeout-ms* 1000) ; how long a Tab waits for the server before giving up
(def *lsp-symbol-timeout-ms* 3000) ; how long M-g i (imenu) waits — symbols can index slower
;; How long the handshake waits for the `initialize` response before giving up on it.
;;
;; Three minutes, where this was ten seconds — because a real language server's
;; `initialize` is not a handshake, it is a BUILD. Expert compiles and indexes the whole
;; mix project before it answers; rust-analyzer does the same with cargo. At ten seconds
;; the client gave up, sent `initialized` to a server that had not initialized, and every
;; request after it was answered by nobody: an Elixir buffer with a correctly configured,
;; correctly installed server that silently never completed anything.
;;
;; A generous bound costs nothing here: this wait is inside the CONNECTION process, so a
;; server that never replies at all delays its own first answer and blocks neither the
;; editor nor any other connection.
(def *lsp-init-timeout-ms* 180000)
;; How much of a server's log line is worth showing. The echo area is one row, and a
;; server's log is written for a log file — Expert's startup narration includes the whole
;; `PATH` and a fifteen-line list of ebin directories.
(def *lsp-notice-width* 110)
(defdyn *lsp-server-specs*
  ;; The language servers bedit knows, in priority order. A file reaches EVERY entry whose
  ;; `:extensions` hold its extension, so one file can have a main server and an add-on
  ;; beside it (ruff next to pyright); the first is the one a lookup asks first.
  ;;
  ;;   :name           how the server is shown and filed
  ;;   :extensions     the file types it serves
  ;;   :commands       `[command args]` alternatives, the first installed one runs
  ;;   :root-markers   files that mark its project root, most decisive first: the root is
  ;;                   the nearest folder above the file holding the first marker found
  ;;                   (`Cargo.lock` names a cargo WORKSPACE, `Cargo.toml` only a crate)
  ;;   :shared         one process may serve several projects (workspace folders), when the
  ;;                   server says it can and the projects pin the same toolchain
  ;;   :add-on         answers beside a main server rather than instead of one
  ;;
  ;; Rebind or extend live to add a server.
  [{:name "brood-lsp"
    :extensions ["blsp"]
    :commands [["brood-lsp" []]]
    :root-markers ["project.blsp"]}
    {:name "rust-analyzer"
     :extensions ["rs"]
     :commands [["rust-analyzer" []]]
     :root-markers ["Cargo.lock" "Cargo.toml"]
     :shared true}
    ;; Expert (expert-lsp.org) — the official Elixir language server, and the one the
    ;; ElixirLS, Lexical and Next LS efforts converged into. `--stdio` because that is this
    ;; client's only transport. Not shared: it builds the project with that project's mix.
    {:name "expert"
     :extensions ["ex" "exs" "heex"]
     :commands [["expert" ["--stdio"]]]
     :root-markers ["mix.lock" "mix.exs"]}
    {:name "ruby-lsp"
     :extensions ["rb" "rake" "gemspec"]
     :commands [["ruby-lsp" []] ["solargraph" ["stdio"]]]
     :root-markers ["Gemfile.lock" "Gemfile"]}
    {:name "python"
     :extensions ["py" "pyi"]
     :commands [["basedpyright-langserver" ["--stdio"]]
                 ["pyright-langserver" ["--stdio"]]
                 ["pylsp" []]]
     :root-markers ["pyproject.toml" "setup.py" "setup.cfg" "requirements.txt"]
     :shared true}
    {:name "ruff"
     :extensions ["py" "pyi"]
     :commands [["ruff" ["server"]]]
     :root-markers ["pyproject.toml" "ruff.toml" ".ruff.toml"]
     :add-on true}
    {:name "gopls"
     :extensions ["go"]
     :commands [["gopls" []]]
     :root-markers ["go.work" "go.mod"]
     :shared true}
    {:name "typescript"
     :extensions ["ts" "tsx" "mts" "cts" "js" "jsx" "mjs" "cjs"]
     :commands [["typescript-language-server" ["--stdio"]]]
     :root-markers ["tsconfig.json" "jsconfig.json" "package.json"]
     :shared true}
    {:name "clangd"
     :extensions ["c" "h" "cc" "cpp" "cxx" "hh" "hpp" "hxx"]
     :commands [["clangd" []]]
     :root-markers ["compile_commands.json" ".clangd" "CMakeLists.txt" "Makefile"]}
    ;; HashiCorp's terraform-ls, or OpenTofu's fork of it — `serve` is stdio. A root module is
    ;; the folder `terraform init` ran in (its lock file, its `.terraform`); without one the
    ;; server starts in the project, which it indexes whole. Shared: one process indexes
    ;; many root modules, each a workspace folder.
    {:name "terraform-ls"
     :extensions ["tf" "tfvars" "tofu"]
     :commands [["terraform-ls" ["serve"]] ["tofu-ls" ["serve"]]]
     :root-markers [".terraform.lock.hcl" ".terraform"]
     :shared true}
    ;; csharp-ls, started where the solution is: it loads the `.sln` (else the `.csproj`) it
    ;; finds in the folder it starts in. A solution's or project's NAME is the project's, so
    ;; the markers are suffixes (`*.sln`, `lsp-marker-in?`) rather than file names.
    {:name "csharp-ls"
     :extensions ["cs" "csx"]
     :commands [["csharp-ls" []]]
     :root-markers ["*.sln" "*.slnx" "*.csproj"]}
    ;; the language servers VS Code ships for CSS and HTML (vscode-langservers-extracted);
    ;; neither builds anything, so a page's folder or its project is root enough
    {:name "css"
     :extensions ["css" "scss" "less"]
     :commands [["vscode-css-language-server" ["--stdio"]]]
     :root-markers ["package.json"]}
    {:name "html"
     :extensions ["html" "htm"]
     :commands [["vscode-html-language-server" ["--stdio"]]]
     :root-markers ["package.json"]}])

(defn lsp-marker-in? (dir marker)
  "True when folder `dir` holds root marker `marker`: a file of that name, or — for a marker
`*.sln` — any entry whose name ends `.sln`, since a solution file is named after its project.
A folder that cannot be listed holds nothing.

    (lsp/lsp-marker-in? \"/nonexistent\" \"*.sln\")   ;=> false"
  (if (string/starts-with? marker "*")
    (let (suffix (string/substring marker 1 (string/length marker)))
      (and (file/dir? dir)
        ;; `file/ls` raises on a folder it may not list: that holds nothing, as said above
        (any? (check-allow :discarded-catch (try (file/ls dir) (catch e '())))
          (fn (name) (string/ends-with? name suffix)))))
    (file/exists? (path/join dir marker))))

;; ---- the wire ---------------------------------------------------------------
;; JSON-RPC over `Content-Length` framing is std's `jsonrpc` — the builders, the frame, and
;; the byte-accurate stream decoder (a body is cut by UTF-8 bytes, and sliced natively,
;; both of which this file learned the hard way before the module existed).
;;
;; The protocol runs BOTH ways, and the half a client forgets is the server's requests. A
;; server may ask the client things — `client/registerCapability` is the common one, and
;; every server that registers its file watchers dynamically sends it — and it is a
;; REQUEST, so the server waits for the answer. Never answering does not degrade anything
;; gracefully: the server blocks, and from outside it looks like a server that initialised
;; perfectly and then ignored every request, which is exactly how Expert appeared.

(def *lsp-server-requests-handled*
  ;; Server→client requests this client answers with a plain `null` result — an
  ;; acknowledgement, which is what the protocol specifies for each. Registration is
  ;; genuinely a no-op here: this client has no dynamic capability table and no file
  ;; watcher, so "registered" and "ignored" are the same state — but SAYING so is not
  ;; optional, because the server is blocked until we do.
  #{"client/registerCapability"
    "client/unregisterCapability" "workspace/semanticTokens/refresh" "workspace/codeLens/refresh"
    "workspace/inlayHint/refresh" "workspace/diagnostic/refresh"
    ;; we advertise `window.workDoneProgress`, so a server asks before it reports progress
    ;; on a token of its own; the token needs no bookkeeping here — every `$/progress` is
    ;; narrated by what it says, whatever token it carries
    "window/workDoneProgress/create"})

;; ---- pure protocol: completion-result shapes --------------------------------

;; An item's LABEL is what the popup would show, not what goes in the buffer, and a server
;; that answers for `XB.T` labels the module `Tool` and the function `call/2`. What it means
;; to insert is its `textEdit` (new text over a range of the line), else its `insertText`,
;; else the label — and either may be a SNIPPET. Read as labels, Tab on `XB.T` replaced the
;; whole word with `Tool`. So an item becomes an ENTRY, `{:text :column}` — the plain text to
;; insert and the UTF-16 column its edit starts at (nil without an edit) — and the source,
;; which knows the line, turns each into a candidate for the whole word before point
;; ([[lsp-completion-candidate]]).

(defn lsp-snippet-text (snippet)
  "The plain text a snippet `insertText` inserts up to its first tab stop or placeholder,
its `\\$`, `\\}` and `\\\\` escapes resolved — `call(${1:a}, ${2:b})` is `call(`.

bedit expands no snippets (it advertises `snippetSupport false`, which a server may ignore).
Stopping at the first stop puts point where the snippet would have: inside the call, ready
for its arguments. The placeholder's own text (`a`) is a NAME to type over, and inserted as
code it would be wrong code.

    (lsp/lsp-snippet-text \"call(${1:a}, ${2:b})\")   ;=> \"call(\"
    (lsp/lsp-snippet-text \"Tool\")   ;=> \"Tool\"
    (lsp/lsp-snippet-text \"cost\\\\$$0\")   ;=> \"cost$\""
  (letrec (scan (fn (chars acc)
                  (cond
                    (empty? chars) acc
                    (= (first chars) "$") acc
                    (and (= (first chars) "\\") (contains? #{"$" "}" "\\"} (second chars)))
                      (scan (rest (rest chars)) (str acc (second chars)))
                    :else (scan (rest chars) (str acc (first chars))))))
    (scan (string/->list snippet) "")))

(defn- lsp-completion-entry (item)
  ;; `{:text :column}` for one CompletionItem, or nil when it inserts nothing. A `textEdit`
  ;; is a TextEdit (`:range`) or an InsertReplaceEdit (`:insert` / `:replace`); its range is
  ;; on the cursor's line, ending at point, so only where it STARTS is needed
  (let (edit (:textEdit item)
        raw (cond
              edit (:newText edit)
              (string? (:insertText item)) (:insertText item)
              :else (:label item))
        text (cond
               (not (string? raw)) nil
               (= (:insertTextFormat item) 2) (lsp-snippet-text raw)
               :else raw)
        column (when edit (map/get-in edit [(if (:range edit) :range :insert) :start :character])))
    (when (and text (not (= text "")))
      {:text text :column (when (int? column) column)})))

(defn lsp-completion-entries (result)
  "The entries `{:text :column}` of a `textDocument/completion` `result` — a
`CompletionItem[]` (a vector), a `{:items [...] :isIncomplete b}` object, or `nil`: each
item's text to insert (its `textEdit`, else `insertText`, else `label`, a snippet cut at its
first stop by [[lsp-snippet-text]]) and the UTF-16 column its edit starts at, nil without
one. What [[lsp-completion-candidate]] turns into a candidate."
  (let (items (cond
                (nil? result) '()
                (vector? result) result
                :else (:items result '())))
    (seq/distinct (seq/keep items lsp-completion-entry))))

(defn lsp-completion-answer (result)
  "`{:entries :incomplete}` for a `textDocument/completion` `result`: the entries
([[lsp-completion-entries]]) and whether the server said the list is NOT the whole answer
(`isIncomplete`) — in which case typing more must ask it again, where a complete list can
be narrowed by filtering it (`complete-at-point`'s cache). A bare `CompletionItem[]` is
complete by the protocol's definition."
  {:entries (lsp-completion-entries result)
   :incomplete (and (map? result) (= true (:isIncomplete result)))})

(defn- lsp-utf16->index (s units)
  ;; the char index in `s` at which `units` UTF-16 code units have passed (LSP's column
  ;; unit: a character beyond the Basic Multilingual Plane is two), capped at its length
  (first (fold (string/->codepoints s)
           [0 0]
           (fn (acc code)
             (let ([index seen] acc)
               (if (>= seen units) acc [(inc index) (+ seen (if (> code 65535) 2 1))]))))))

(defn- lsp-prefix-qualifier (prefix separators)
  ;; `prefix` up to and including its last separator — `XB.` of `XB.T` — or ""
  (letrec (back (fn (i)
                  (cond
                    (< i 0) ""
                    (contains? separators (string/char-at prefix i))
                      (string/substring prefix 0 (inc i))
                    :else (back (dec i)))))
    (back (dec (string/length prefix)))))

(defn lsp-completion-candidate (line start entry separators)
  "The whole word an `entry` ([[lsp-completion-entries]]) completes the word before point
to, or nil when it cannot say — `line` the cursor's line up to point, `start` where the word
begins in it, `separators` the buffer's segment separators. Every completion source answers
whole words from `start`, which is what lets them merge and Tab step a segment at a time.

- An edit starting at or after `start` keeps the line between them: `Tool` over the `T` of
  `XB.T` is `XB.Tool`. One starting before `start` must begin with the text it rewrites.
- Without an edit, text extending the word is the word; any other replaces the word's last
  segment, as a client whose word stops at `.` would read it: `Tool` for `XB.T` is
  `XB.Tool`, `call/2` for `XB.Tool.c` is `XB.Tool.call/2`, which Tab stops before the
  `/2` of.

    (lsp/lsp-completion-candidate \"  XB.T\" 2 {:text \"Tool\" :column 5} #{\".\"})   ;=> \"XB.Tool\"
    (lsp/lsp-completion-candidate \"XB.T\" 0 {:text \"Tool\" :column nil} #{\".\"})   ;=> \"XB.Tool\"
    (lsp/lsp-completion-candidate \"XB.T\" 0 {:text \"XB.Tool\" :column nil} #{\".\"})   ;=> \"XB.Tool\""
  (let (text (:text entry)
        column (:column entry)
        prefix (string/substring line start (string/length line)))
    (if (nil? column)
      (if (string/starts-with? text prefix)
        text
        (str (lsp-prefix-qualifier prefix separators) text))
      (let (from (lsp-utf16->index line column))
        (if (>= from start)
          (str (string/substring line start from) text)
          (let (lead (string/substring line from start))
            (when (string/starts-with? text lead)
              (string/substring text (string/length lead) (string/length text)))))))))

;; ---- pure protocol: documentSymbol shapes (imenu, M-g i) --------------------
;; `textDocument/documentSymbol` returns either a flat `SymbolInformation[]` (each with a
;; `:location {:range …}`) or a hierarchical `DocumentSymbol[]` (each with `:range` +
;; `:selectionRange` and nested `:children`). We flatten both to a list of
;; `{:name :kind :line :char}`, qualifying a child's name with its parent's (Emacs-style
;; `Class.method`) so the imenu list is unique and readable. Positions are 0-based
;; (LSP units); the command converts them to a buffer offset.

(def *lsp-symbol-kinds*
  ;; LSP SymbolKind (integer) → short display tag. Unmapped kinds show "?".
  {1 "file"
   2 "module"
   3 "namespace"
   4 "package"
   5 "class"
   6 "method"
   7 "property"
   8 "field"
   9 "ctor"
   10 "seq"
   11 "interface"
   12 "fn"
   13 "var"
   14 "const"
   15 "string"
   16 "number"
   17 "bool"
   18 "array"
   19 "object"
   20 "key"
   21 "null"
   22 "enum-member"
   23 "struct"
   24 "event"
   25 "operator"
   26 "type-param"})

(defn lsp-symbol-kind-name (kind)
  "The short display tag for an LSP SymbolKind integer (`*lsp-symbol-kinds*`), or \"?\"."
  (get *lsp-symbol-kinds* kind "?"))

(defn- lsp-symbol-pos (sym)
  "The 0-based `[line char]` to jump to for `sym` — its `:selectionRange` start (a
hierarchical DocumentSymbol), else its `:range` start, else its `:location`'s range start
(a flat SymbolInformation). `[0 0]` if none is present."
  (let (span (or (:selectionRange sym) (:range sym) (map/get-in sym [:location :range]))
        start (when span (:start span)))
    [(:line start 0) (:character start 0)]))

(defn- lsp-flatten-symbols (syms prefix acc)
  "Fold `syms` (a DocumentSymbol/SymbolInformation vector) into `acc` as `{:name :kind
:line :char}` entries, depth-first, qualifying each child's name with `prefix` (its parent
chain). `acc` is built reversed."
  (fold syms
    acc
    (fn (a sym)
      (let ([line char] (lsp-symbol-pos sym)
            qname (if (= prefix "") (:name sym) (str prefix "." (:name sym)))
            entry {:name qname :kind (:kind sym) :line line :char char})
        (lsp-flatten-symbols (:children sym []) qname (cons entry a))))))

(defn lsp-document-symbols (result)
  "The `textDocument/documentSymbol` `result` (a vector of DocumentSymbol or
SymbolInformation, or nil) flattened to a list of `{:name :kind :line :char}`, parents
before children, in document order."
  (if (nil? result) '() (reverse (lsp-flatten-symbols result "" '()))))

;; ---- pure protocol: location / hover / edit shapes (goto, refs, hover, rename) ----

(defn lsp-uri->path (uri)
  "The filesystem path of a `file://` `uri`, percent-decoded (the inverse of
`lsp-path->uri`); `uri` unchanged if it has no scheme, and the raw path if it does not
decode."
  (if (string/starts-with? uri "file://")
    (let (raw (string/substring uri 7 (string/length uri))
          decoded (url/percent-decode raw))
      (if (string? decoded) decoded raw))
    uri))

(defn- lsp-location (loc)
  "Normalise one LSP `Location` (`:uri`+`:range`) or `LocationLink` (`:targetUri`+
`:targetSelectionRange`/`:targetRange`) to `{:path :line :char}` (0-based)."
  (let (uri (or (:uri loc) (:targetUri loc) "")
        span (or (:range loc) (:targetSelectionRange loc) (:targetRange loc))
        start (when span (:start span)))
    {:path (lsp-uri->path uri) :line (:line start 0) :char (:character start 0)}))

(defn lsp-locations (result)
  "The `textDocument/definition`/`references` `result` — a single `Location`/`LocationLink`,
a vector of them, or nil — as a list of `{:path :line :char}`."
  (cond
    (nil? result) '()
    (vector? result) (map result lsp-location)
    :else (list (lsp-location result))))

(defn- lsp-marked (x)
  "The text of one hover content item — a plain string, or a `{:value …}` (MarkupContent /
MarkedString object)."
  (cond (string? x) x (map? x) (:value x "") :else ""))

(defn lsp-hover-text (result)
  "The plain text of a `textDocument/hover` `result` — its `:contents`, which is a string, a
`{:value …}`, or a vector of either (joined by blank lines). `\"\"` when empty/nil."
  (if (nil? result)
    ""
    (let (c (:contents result))
      (cond
        (nil? c) ""
        (string? c) c
        (vector? c) (string/join (seq/reject (map c lsp-marked) empty?) "\n\n")
        :else (lsp-marked c)))))

;; ---- pure protocol: signature help (the argument hints as you type) ----------
;; `textDocument/signatureHelp` is `{:signatures [{:label :parameters [{:label …}]}]
;;  :activeSignature :activeParameter}`. What the reader wants out of it is one line —
;; the signature being called, with the argument the cursor is sitting on picked out —
;; and that is the whole of what this produces.

(def *signature-mark* ["‹" "›"])
;; The active parameter is MARKED rather than faced: the echo row is one `text` op with
;; one face (see `view/ed-echo-line`), so there is nowhere to put a highlight. Guillemets
;; because they are light enough to read through and rare enough in source that
;; `‹conn›` is unambiguously our mark and not part of the signature.

(defn- lsp-param-span (label param)
  "The `[start end]` character span of `param` (a `SignatureInformation.parameters` entry)
within the signature's `label` — the protocol's own offsets when it gives them, else the
first occurrence of the parameter's own label text. Nil when neither locates it.

Both forms are in the wild and a client has to read both: Expert and rust-analyzer send
offsets, gopls and several TypeScript servers send the substring."
  (let (l (:label param))
    (cond
      (vector? l) (when (= (count l) 2) [(first l) (second l)])
      (string? l)
        (let (i (index-of label l))
          (when i [i (+ i (string/length l))]))
      else nil)))

(defn- lsp-mark-param (label span)
  "`label` with the characters in `span` wrapped in [[*signature-mark*]]; `label`
unchanged when `span` is nil or does not lie inside it."
  (let ([open close] *signature-mark*
        n (string/length label))
    (if (nil? span)
      label
      (let ([s e] span)
        (if (or (< s 0) (> e n) (>= s e))
          label
          (str (string/substring label 0 s)
            open
            (string/substring label s e)
            close
            (string/substring label e n)))))))

(defn lsp-signature-text (result)
  "The one line a `textDocument/signatureHelp` `result` is worth showing — the active
signature's label with the active parameter marked — or `\"\"` when the server has nothing
to say (which is its answer whenever point is not inside a call).

`activeParameter` is read from the signature first and the help second: LSP 3.16 let a
`SignatureInformation` carry its own, and a server that sends both means the inner one."
  (if (nil? result)
    ""
    (let (sigs (or (:signatures result) [])
          i (or (:activeSignature result) 0)
          sig (nth sigs (if (and (>= i 0) (< i (count sigs))) i 0) nil))
      (if (nil? sig)
        ""
        (let (label (str (:label sig ""))
              params (or (:parameters sig) [])
              active (or (:activeParameter sig) (:activeParameter result))
              param (when (and active (>= active 0) (< active (count params))) (nth params active))
              ;; more than one signature is worth knowing about — otherwise the reader has
              ;; no way to tell that the arity they are typing towards is not the only one
              more (if (> (count sigs) 1) (str "  (" (+ 1 i) "/" (count sigs) ")") ""))
          (str (if param (lsp-mark-param label (lsp-param-span label param)) label) more))))))

;; ---- pure protocol: code actions (the fix-it list at point) -------------------
;; `textDocument/codeAction` answers a list whose entries are either a `Command`
;; (`{:title :command :arguments}` — ask the server to do it) or a `CodeAction`
;; (`{:title :kind :edit :command}` — edits we apply ourselves, a command, or both).
;; Normalised here to one shape so the editor side never has to tell them apart.

(defn lsp-code-action-list (result)
  "The `textDocument/codeAction` `result` as a list of
`{:title :kind :edit :command :arguments}` — `:edit` a normalised workspace edit (nil when
the action carries none) and `:command`/`:arguments` the server-side call to make instead
(nil when it carries edits).

An action with neither is dropped: it is a title with nothing behind it, and offering it
in the picker would promise a fix that cannot happen."
  (if (nil? result)
    '()
    (seq/keep result
      (fn (a)
        (let (cmd (:command a)
              ;; a `Command`'s own `:command` is a string; a `CodeAction`'s is a nested
              ;; Command object — the one field that tells the two forms apart
              [name args] (cond
                            (string? cmd) [cmd (or (:arguments a) [])]
                            (map? cmd) [(:command cmd) (or (:arguments cmd) [])]
                            else [nil []])
              edit (when (:edit a) (lsp-workspace-edit (:edit a))))
          (when (or name (and edit (not (empty? edit))))
            {:title (str (:title a ""))
             :kind (:kind a)
             :edit edit
             :command name
             :arguments args}))))))

(defn- lsp-text-edit (e)
  "Normalise one `TextEdit` (`:range {:start :end}` + `:newText`) to
`{:start-line :start-char :end-line :end-char :text}` (0-based)."
  (let (span (:range e) s (:start span) en (:end span))
    {:start-line (:line s 0)
     :start-char (:character s 0)
     :end-line (:line en 0)
     :end-char (:character en 0)
     :text (:newText e "")}))

(defn lsp-text-edits (result)
  "A `TextEdit[]` (`textDocument/formatting`, or null) as a list of normalised edits
(`lsp-text-edit`)."
  (if (nil? result) '() (map result lsp-text-edit)))

(defn lsp-workspace-edit (result)
  "A `WorkspaceEdit` (`textDocument/rename` result) as `{path -> [edit …]}` (paths, not
uris; edits normalised). Handles both the `:changes` map and the `:documentChanges` array
shapes; `{}` when nil."
  (if (nil? result)
    {}
    (let (changes (:changes result)
          docs (:documentChanges result))
      (cond
        changes
        ;; JSON object keys parse to keywords, so a `:changes` uri arrives as `:file:///a`,
        ;; not a string — coerce back with `->string` before stripping the scheme.
          (reduce (keys changes)
            {}
            (fn (acc uri)
              (assoc acc
                (lsp-uri->path (if (keyword? uri) (->string uri) uri))
                  (map (get changes uri) lsp-text-edit))))
        docs
          (reduce docs
            {}
            (fn (acc dc)
              (assoc acc
                (lsp-uri->path (map/get-in dc [:textDocument :uri])) (map (:edits dc) lsp-text-edit))))
        :else {}))))

;; ---- pure connection logic (state -> [state effects]) -----------------------
;; The connection process's state:
;;   {:proc handle :buf "" :next-id N :pending {jsonrpc-id -> [reply-to corr]}
;;    :opened {uri -> true} :version V}
;; `lsp-ingest` and `lsp-issue` are pure transitions returning the new
;; state plus the IO the shell must perform (messages to send / frames to write), so
;; correlation and framing are testable without a process or a server.

(defn- lsp-did-open (uri lang text)
  (jsonrpc/notification "textDocument/didOpen"
    {:textDocument {:uri uri :languageId lang :version 1 :text text}}))

(defn lsp-close-document (state uri)
  "Pure: the server stops being told about `uri` (its buffer is gone, or its project left
the server): `didClose` when it was opened, and the uri forgotten, so a later sync opens it
afresh. `[state' frames]`."
  (if (get (:opened state) uri)
    [(assoc state :opened (dissoc (:opened state) uri) :tokens (dissoc (or (:tokens state) {}) uri))
      [(jsonrpc/notification "textDocument/didClose" {:textDocument {:uri uri}})]]
    [state []]))

(defn- lsp-did-change (uri version changes)
  ;; `changes` is the `contentChanges` vector: one `{:text}` for the whole document, or
  ;; ranged `{:range :text}` edits applied in order
  (jsonrpc/notification "textDocument/didChange"
    {:textDocument {:uri uri :version version} :contentChanges changes}))

;; ---- the document sync: whole text, or the edits since the last one ---------------
;; Sending the document is what made a Tab cost a second on a big file: the connection
;; JSON-encodes all of it (341 ms for a 44k-line buffer, measured) and the server re-reads
;; all of it, on every completion request and every idle beat after an edit. The buffer
;; already logs what each edit DID (`buffer-splices-since`), so the editor can send that
;; instead — a keystroke is a one-character change, whatever the file's size.
;;
;; Changes only make sense against the text the server HAS, and only the connection knows
;; what that is. So every sync carries a TOKEN (a fresh ref) naming the text it leaves the
;; server holding, and an incremental one names its BASE; the connection applies it only
;; when the base is the token it holds for that uri. Anything else — a reconnected server,
;; a full sync a lookup made meanwhile, a model the editor threw away after syncing — is
;; refused, and the editor is told (`[:lsp-resync uri conn]`) to send the whole text next time.
;; A refused change costs one full sync; an applied wrong one would corrupt the server's
;; copy of the file until it was reopened.

(defn lsp-incremental-sync? (caps)
  "Does a server with capabilities `caps` accept ranged `didChange` edits — a
`textDocumentSync` of 2 (Incremental), as a number or as `{:change 2}`?"
  (let (sync (get (or caps {}) :textDocumentSync))
    (= 2 (if (map? sync) (get sync :change) sync))))

(defn- lsp-utf16-length (s)
  "`s`'s length in UTF-16 code units — LSP's column unit: a character beyond the Basic
Multilingual Plane (an emoji) is two."
  (fold (string/->codepoints s) 0 (fn (acc code) (+ acc (if (> code 65535) 2 1)))))

(defn- lsp-rope-position (rope offset)
  "The LSP `{:line :character}` of char `offset` in `rope`."
  (let (line (text/char->line rope offset)
        line-start (text/line->char rope line))
    (lsp-pos line (lsp-utf16-length (text/slice rope line-start offset)))))

(defn lsp-content-changes (before after)
  "The ranged `contentChanges` that turn buffer `before`'s text into `after`'s — one
`{:range {:start :end} :text}` per edit the buffer logged, in order, each positioned
against the text the previous one left (which is how LSP applies them) — or nil when the
log cannot account for the change (an undo snapshot, a wholesale replace, more edits than
it keeps: `buffer-splices-since`), for the caller to send the whole text instead."
  (let (splices (buffer-splices-since before after))
    (when (not (empty? splices))
      (second (fold splices
                [(buffer-rope before) []]
                (fn (acc splice)
                  (let ([rope changes] acc
                        [lo hi replacement] splice)
                    [(text/insert (text/delete rope lo hi) lo replacement)
                      (conj changes
                        {:range {:start (lsp-rope-position rope lo)
                                 :end (lsp-rope-position rope hi)}
                         :text replacement})])))))))

(defn- lsp-sync-document (state uri lang sync)
  "Pure: bring the server's copy of `uri` up to date. `sync` is `{:text :token}` — the whole
document: `didOpen` the first time a uri is seen (tagged with `lang`, the LSP languageId),
else a full-text `didChange` — or `{:changes :base :token}`, ranged edits against the text
that `:base` names ([[lsp-content-changes]]), applied only when that is the text the server
holds and refused otherwise (no frame; the uri is queued under `:resyncs` for the shell to
report). Either way the uri then holds `:token` (nil for a sync nobody will build on).
Returns `[state' frames]`. The one sync behind both a bare `:sync` and every request about a
document."
  (let (opened (:opened state)
        version (:version state)
        tokens (or (:tokens state) {})
        changes (:changes sync))
    (cond
      (nil? changes)
        [(assoc state
           :version (+ version 1)
           :opened (assoc opened uri true)
           :tokens (assoc tokens uri (:token sync)))
          [(if (get opened uri)
             (lsp-did-change uri version [{:text (:text sync)}])
             (lsp-did-open uri lang (:text sync)))]]
      (and (get opened uri) (not (nil? (:base sync))) (= (get tokens uri) (:base sync)))
        [(assoc state :version (+ version 1) :tokens (assoc tokens uri (:token sync)))
          [(lsp-did-change uri version changes)]]
      :else
        [(assoc state
           :tokens (dissoc tokens uri)
           :resyncs (conj (or (:resyncs state) []) uri))
          []])))

;; ---- what each request kind puts on the wire ------------------------------------
;; The `LspRequest` method/params halves (see `lsp-requests`). A REQUEST is the map the
;; editor sent — `{:uri :lang :text :line :char}` for a question about the text at point,
;; plus whatever one kind needs (`:diags`, `:new-name`), or `{:command :arguments}` for one
;; that is about no document at all.

(defn- lsp-pos (line char) {:line line :character char})

(defn- lsp-doc (request) {:textDocument {:uri (:uri request)}})

(defn- lsp-doc-at (request)
  ;; `{:textDocument {:uri …}}` plus the `:position` every cursor-located request carries
  (assoc (lsp-doc request) :position (lsp-pos (:line request) (:char request))))

(defn- lsp-request-frame (id kind request)
  "The framed JSON-RPC request for `kind` (an `LspRequest` record) built from `request`."
  (jsonrpc/request id (lsp-request-method kind) (lsp-request-params kind request)))

(defn- lsp-issue (state corr reply-to kind request)
  "Pure: the frames + next state for one request of `kind` (an `LspRequest` record). A
request carrying the document's `:text` syncs it first ([[lsp-sync-document]]) — the
editor's own requests carry none, having synced incrementally just before asking
(`lsp-commands/ed-lsp-push-text`), so asking costs the request and not the document;
then the request itself ([[lsp-request-frame]]), recording `[reply-to corr kind]` under
its jsonrpc id so the response is routed back and shaped per `kind` (`lsp-shape`). The one
transition behind every request — completion, symbols, definition, references, hover,
signature, code actions, commands, formatting, rename. Returns `[state' frames]`."
  (let ([synced sync-frames] (if (and (contains? request :uri) (contains? request :text))
                               (lsp-sync-document state
                                 (:uri request)
                                 (:lang request)
                                 {:text (:text request) :token nil})
                               [state []])
        id (:next-id synced)
        state2 (assoc synced
                 :next-id (+ id 1)
                 :pending (assoc (:pending synced) id [reply-to corr kind])))
    [state2 (conj sync-frames (lsp-request-frame id kind request))]))

;; The connection's half of the `LspRequest` ability (see `lsp-requests`), one impl per
;; kind, beside the shaping helpers: what goes on the wire, and the raw server result ->
;; `[reply-tag value]`. The ids are written qualified — a bare id would qualify to THIS
;; module.
(impl LspRequest
  lsp-requests/lsp-completion
  (lsp-request-method (_) "textDocument/completion")
  (lsp-request-params (_ request) (lsp-doc-at request))
  (lsp-request-shape (_ result) [:lsp-completion (lsp-completion-answer result)]))
(impl LspRequest
  lsp-requests/lsp-symbols
  (lsp-request-method (_) "textDocument/documentSymbol")
  (lsp-request-params (_ request) (lsp-doc request))
  (lsp-request-shape (_ result) [:lsp-symbols (lsp-document-symbols result)]))
(impl LspRequest
  lsp-requests/lsp-definition
  (lsp-request-method (_) "textDocument/definition")
  (lsp-request-params (_ request) (lsp-doc-at request))
  (lsp-request-shape (_ result) [:lsp-definition (lsp-locations result)]))
(impl LspRequest
  lsp-requests/lsp-references
  (lsp-request-method (_) "textDocument/references")
  (lsp-request-params (_ request)
    (assoc (lsp-doc-at request) :context {:includeDeclaration true}))
  (lsp-request-shape (_ result) [:lsp-references (lsp-locations result)]))
(impl LspRequest
  lsp-requests/lsp-hover
  (lsp-request-method (_) "textDocument/hover")
  (lsp-request-params (_ request) (lsp-doc-at request))
  (lsp-request-shape (_ result) [:lsp-hover (lsp-hover-text result)]))
(impl LspRequest
  lsp-requests/lsp-signature
  (lsp-request-method (_) "textDocument/signatureHelp")
  (lsp-request-params (_ request) (lsp-doc-at request))
  (lsp-request-shape (_ result) [:lsp-signature (lsp-signature-text result)]))
(impl LspRequest
  lsp-requests/lsp-code-actions
  (lsp-request-method (_) "textDocument/codeAction")
  (lsp-request-params (_ request)
    (let (at (lsp-pos (:line request) (:char request)))
      (assoc (lsp-doc request)
        ;; an empty range AT point: the actions offered for where the cursor is. (A region
        ;; would widen it to "extract this" territory — a later feature, not a wider range
        ;; smuggled in here.)
        :range {:start at :end at}
        ;; the diagnostics on the line go WITH it — how a server knows which quickfix
        :context {:diagnostics (or (:diags request) [])})))
  (lsp-request-shape (_ result) [:lsp-code-actions (lsp-code-action-list result)]))
(impl LspRequest
  lsp-requests/lsp-execute-command
  ;; no document: the server is being asked to run something it already offered, not to
  ;; look at text — so the request carries no `:uri` and `lsp-issue` syncs nothing
  (lsp-request-method (_) "workspace/executeCommand")
  (lsp-request-params (_ request)
    {:command (:command request) :arguments (or (:arguments request) [])})
  ;; `workspace/executeCommand` answers with whatever the command returns, usually null:
  ;; the EDITS come separately, as a `workspace/applyEdit` request from the server
  ;; ([[lsp-answer-server]]). So this reply is only ever "it ran".
  (lsp-request-shape (_ result) [:lsp-command-done result]))
(impl LspRequest
  lsp-requests/lsp-formatting
  (lsp-request-method (_) "textDocument/formatting")
  (lsp-request-params (_ request)
    (assoc (lsp-doc request) :options {:tabSize 2 :insertSpaces true}))
  (lsp-request-shape (_ result) [:lsp-text-edits (lsp-text-edits result)]))
(impl LspRequest
  lsp-requests/lsp-rename
  (lsp-request-method (_) "textDocument/rename")
  (lsp-request-params (_ request) (assoc (lsp-doc-at request) :newName (:new-name request)))
  (lsp-request-shape (_ result) [:lsp-workspace-edit (lsp-workspace-edit result)]))

(defn- lsp-shape (kind result)
  "The `[reply-tag value]` for a response of the given request `kind` (an `LspRequest`
record — `lsp-request-shape`), shaping the raw `result` into what the waiting command
wants."
  (lsp-request-shape kind result))

(def *lsp-message-levels*
  ;; LSP's `MessageType`, the `type` of a `window/logMessage` or `window/showMessage`.
  ;; A word rather than the number, because the word is what the log line says.
  {1 "error" 2 "warning" 3 "info" 4 "log" 5 "debug"})

(def *lsp-echoed-show-levels*
  ;; Which `window/showMessage` levels reach the echo area. `showMessage` is the server
  ;; ASKING for the reader's attention, so every level that is a sentence for a person does;
  ;; `log` and `debug` are the protocol's own name for "not for the reader".
  #{"error" "warning" "info"})

(defn lsp-notice-line (raw)
  "The ONE line of a server's message `raw` worth an echo area, clipped to
`*lsp-notice-width*` — or nil for one that says nothing.

A server's log is written for a log file: Expert's startup includes the whole `PATH` and a
fifteen-line list of ebin directories, and the echo area is one row. The first line is the
sentence; the rest is the attachment, kept whole in `*lsp-log*`."
  (when raw
    (let (line (first (string/split (str raw) "\n")))
      (when (not (= (string/trim line) ""))
        (if (> (string/length line) *lsp-notice-width*)
          (str (string/substring line 0 (- *lsp-notice-width* 1)) "…")
          line)))))

(defn lsp-notice (msg)
  "What a server SAID, as `{:channel :level :text :line}` — or nil for a message that says
nothing (a progress `end` with no message, any other notification).

`:channel` is which of the protocol's three voices it came in, and it decides where the
words go (see [[lsp-notice-echo?]]):

- `:log` — `window/logMessage`, the server's LOG. Expert forwards its whole Logger here,
  the indexer's `[error] Could not expand alias: {:%, [line: 36 …` included — internal
  noise about the server's own work, at level error, many times a second while it indexes.
- `:show` — `window/showMessage`, the server asking to tell the reader something.
- `:progress` — `$/progress`, work the reader is waiting on. This is how Expert narrates
  its startup (`Starting engine node`): it answers nothing until its engine node is up, and
  with the narration dropped the first half-minute is a server that looks broken.

`:text` is the whole message, for the log; `:line` the one line an echo area can hold."
  (let (params (or (:params msg) {})
        value (or (:value params) {})
        method (:method msg)
        channel (case method
                  "window/logMessage" :log
                  "window/showMessage" :show
                  "$/progress" :progress
                  nil)
        ;; a progress `begin` carries :title, a `report` :message, an `end` either
        raw (if (= channel :progress) (or (:title value) (:message value)) (:message params))
        line (when channel (lsp-notice-line raw)))
    (when line
      {:channel channel
       :level (if (= channel :progress) "info" (get *lsp-message-levels* (:type params) "info"))
       :text (str raw)
       :line line})))

(defn lsp-notice-echo? (notice)
  "Whether a server's `notice` ([[lsp-notice]]) belongs in the echo area, the way eglot
and lsp-mode decide it: progress and a `showMessage` the reader is meant to see do; the
LOG never does, at any level — it goes to `*lsp-log*` only.

Echoing the log is what flooded the echo area while editing Elixir: Expert's indexer logs
an error for every struct literal whose alias it cannot expand, and each one replaced
whatever the reader was looking at — even in a dired buffer nowhere near the file."
  (case (:channel notice)
    :progress true
    :show (contains? *lsp-echoed-show-levels* (:level notice))
    false))

(defn lsp-notice-log-line (notice)
  "The `*lsp-log*` line for a server's `notice`: the channel and level, then the WHOLE
message — the log is where the attachment the echo area clipped off is kept."
  (str "["
    (case (:channel notice) :log "log" :show "show" :progress "progress" "lsp")
    " "
    (:level notice)
    "] "
    (:text notice)))

(def *lsp-severity-labels*
  ;; LSP severities are numbers; bedit's diagnostics carry no severity field, so the word
  ;; goes in the message — which is also how the Brood checker's own findings read
  ;; (`warning: unbound symbol: x`), so one buffer's notes cannot be told from another's
  ;; by their shape.
  {1 "error: " 2 "warning: " 3 "note: " 4 "hint: "})

(defn lsp-diagnostic (d)
  "One LSP `Diagnostic` in bedit's own shape — `{:line :col :message}`, **1-based**, which
is what `model/ed-diag-index` and the view's token underline read. LSP counts lines and
characters from 0, so both are shifted by one; getting that wrong puts every mark one line
above the code it is about."
  (let (start (map/get-in d [:range :start] {})
        sev (get *lsp-severity-labels* (:severity d) ""))
    {:line (inc (or (:line start) 0))
     :col (inc (or (:character start) 0))
     ;; the NUMBER travels too, not only the word baked into the message. Everything that
     ;; has to tell an error from an advice — the margin glyph, the mode-line chip, the
     ;; report row — asks `results/diagnostic-error?`, and with only the prefix to go on it
     ;; called every server finding a warning.
     :severity (:severity d)
     :message (str sev (or (:message d) ""))}))

(defn lsp-diagnostics-notice (params)
  "A `textDocument/publishDiagnostics` payload as `[path items raw]` — the file it is
about, its diagnostics in bedit's shape, and the server's own untouched objects. An empty
list is meaningful and is kept: it is how a server says a file is now CLEAN, and dropping
it leaves the last errors on screen after they have been fixed.

The raw objects are kept because a quickfix is asked for by handing the diagnostic BACK
(`textDocument/codeAction`'s context): a server matches the fix to the finding by the
object it published, so bedit's flattened `{:line :col :message}` — which is all the view
needs — cannot ask for one."
  (let (raw (into [] (or (:diagnostics params) [])))
    [(lsp-uri->path (or (:uri params) "")) (into [] (map raw lsp-diagnostic)) raw]))

(defn lsp-diagnostics-at-line (raw line)
  "The raw LSP diagnostics whose range covers 0-based `line` — the `context.diagnostics`
for a code-action request at point, which is how a server knows which quickfixes to offer."
  (into []
    (seq/filter (or raw [])
      (fn (d)
        (let (start (map/get-in d [:range :start] {})
              end (map/get-in d [:range :end] {}))
          (and (<= (or (:line start) 0) line) (>= (or (:line end) 0) line)))))))

(defn- lsp-opened-path? (state path)
  ;; whether `path` is a document this connection opened, compared DECODED: a server may spell
  ;; a file's URI differently from `lsp-path->uri` (rust-analyzer leaves `+` and `@`
  ;; unescaped), and comparing the raw strings dropped every diagnostic for such a file
  (any? (keys (or (:opened state) {})) (fn (uri) (= (lsp-uri->path uri) path))))

(defn- lsp-publish (state msg)
  "Queue a server's diagnostics for the editor, on the state's `:diags` — only for a document
this connection has OPENED, and only the newest publication per file.

A server indexing a project publishes about files nobody has open — Expert walks every
module of an umbrella app, rust-analyzer its whole workspace — and the editor keeps
diagnostics only for open buffers anyway. Forwarding each one cost the editor a full loop
turn to throw it away, hundreds a second while indexing, and the keys and clicks queued
behind them: the editor froze until the indexer finished. A file opened later is published
again when its `didOpen` arrives, so nothing the reader could see is lost; and within one
batch a later publication for a file replaces the earlier, which says nothing the newer
one does not."
  (let (params (or (:params msg) {})
        notice (lsp-diagnostics-notice params)
        path (first notice))
    (if (not (lsp-opened-path? state path))
      state
      (assoc state
        :diags
          (conj (into [] (seq/reject (or (:diags state) []) (fn (d) (= (first d) path)))) notice)))))

(defn- lsp-queue-notice (state notice)
  "Queue `notice` (a [[lsp-notice]] map) for the editor on the state's `:notices` (the
shell sends it). Kept out of `:outbox`, which is frames for the SERVER — these go the
other way."
  (if (nil? notice) state (assoc state :notices (conj (or (:notices state) []) notice))))

(defn- lsp-narrate (state msg)
  "Queue what a server's notification SAID for the editor ([[lsp-notice]]); the editor
decides whether it reaches the echo area or only the log ([[lsp-notice-echo?]])."
  (lsp-queue-notice state (lsp-notice msg)))

(defn lsp-error-notice (kind error)
  "The `*lsp-log*` notice for a server's error RESPONSE to one of our requests of `kind`
(an `LspRequest` record) — logged, never echoed.

Most requests are not the reader's: the idle beat asks for signature help as point moves,
and a server busy indexing refuses those by the dozen. The waiting command still gets its
reply — an empty result, so a lookup the reader DID ask for says `no definition found` in
its own voice — and the server's reason is kept here, where someone debugging it can read
it, instead of nowhere."
  (let (method (lsp-request-method kind)
        text (str method " failed: " (or (:message error) "") " (code " (:code error) ")"))
    {:channel :log :level "error" :text text :line (lsp-notice-line text)}))

(defn- lsp-apply-edit-request (state msg)
  "A server's `workspace/applyEdit` — the edits a code action's command produced. Queued
for the editor on `:edits`, and answered `{:applied true}` at once.

Answered optimistically, and that is a decision rather than a shortcut: the edit is
applied by the editor loop, which is a different process, so the only honest alternative
is to block this connection until it reports back — and a connection that stops reading
its server mid-action is how a client deadlocks. The edits go through the same rope guard
every other applied edit does, so `true` here means \"accepted\", which is what the field
is worth in every client."
  (let (id (:id msg)
        params (or (:params msg) {})
        edit (lsp-workspace-edit (:edit params)))
    (assoc state
      :edits (append (or (:edits state) []) [edit])
      :outbox (append (or (:outbox state) []) [(jsonrpc/response id {:applied true})]))))

(defn- lsp-answer-server (state msg)
  "Queue this client's answer to a request the SERVER made, on the state's `:outbox` (the
shell writes it). `workspace/applyEdit` carries work and is handled; the rest are
acknowledged; anything else gets MethodNotFound, which is a real answer and unblocks the
server just as well as agreement would."
  (let (id (:id msg)
        method (:method msg))
    (cond
      (= method "workspace/applyEdit") (lsp-apply-edit-request state msg)
      ;; we advertise `workspace.configuration`, so the server asks — one result per item
      ;; it asked about, and `null` is "no setting, use your default". MethodNotFound here
      ;; answered a capability we had claimed.
      (= method "workspace/configuration")
        (assoc state
          :outbox
            (append (or (:outbox state) [])
              [(jsonrpc/response id
                 (into [] (map (map/get-in msg [:params :items] []) (fn (_) nil))))]))
      :else
        (let (frame (if (contains? *lsp-server-requests-handled* method)
                      (jsonrpc/response id nil)
                      (jsonrpc/error-response id
                        (:method-not-found jsonrpc/*error-codes*)
                        (str "bedit does not implement " method))))
          (assoc state :outbox (append (or (:outbox state) []) [frame]))))))

(defn- lsp-dispatch (acc msg)
  ;; Fold step: route one decoded message. Three kinds arrive on this stream and they are
  ;; told apart by which fields they carry:
  ;;
  ;;   :id + :method  a REQUEST from the server — it is waiting on us, so it is answered
  ;;   :id            a response to one of ours — shaped and sent to its waiter
  ;;   :method        a notification (publishDiagnostics, logMessage) — nothing to do
  ;;
  ;; The first was missing entirely, and its absence does not look like a missing feature:
  ;; the server simply stops, because it is blocked on an answer that never comes.
  (let ([state replies] acc
        id (:id msg)
        entry (when id (map/get-in state [:pending id])))
    (cond
      (and id (:method msg)) [(lsp-answer-server state msg) replies]
      ;; a notification: nothing to answer, but the server may be telling us where the
      ;; errors are, or saying something (see [[lsp-notice]] — and [[lsp-notice-echo?]] for
      ;; why most of it is the server's log and never reaches the echo area)
      (= (:method msg) "textDocument/publishDiagnostics") [(lsp-publish state msg) replies]
      (nil? id) [(lsp-narrate state msg) replies]
      (nil? entry) [state replies]
      else
        (let (reply-to (first entry)
              corr (second entry)
              kind (nth entry 2)
              [tag value] (lsp-shape kind (:result msg))
              ;; an error response still answers its waiter (as an empty result); the
              ;; server's reason goes to the log, not the echo area ([[lsp-error-notice]])
              state (if (:error msg)
                      (lsp-queue-notice state (lsp-error-notice kind (:error msg)))
                      state))
          [(assoc state :pending (dissoc (:pending state) id))
            (cons [reply-to [tag corr value]] replies)]))))

(defn- lsp-ingest (state chunk)
  "Pure: fold a stdout `chunk` into the connection. Appends it to the frame buffer,
decodes every complete message, and routes each. Returns `[state' replies]` where each
reply is `[pid message]` for the shell to `send`."
  (let (buf (str (:buf state) chunk)
        [msgs remaining] (jsonrpc/decode buf))
    (fold msgs [(assoc state :buf remaining) '()] lsp-dispatch)))

;; ---- the connection process (the thin IO shell) -----------------------------

(defn- lsp-send-all (proc frames)
  (doseq (f frames) (os/write proc f)))

(def *lsp-flush-ms*
  ;; How long what a server SAID (its log, progress, diagnostics) may wait to be sent to
  ;; the editor as one batch ([[lsp-flush]]). Short enough that a diagnostic still lands
  ;; with the pause that asked for it; long enough that an indexer's hundreds of lines a
  ;; second reach the editor as a few events instead of hundreds.
  100)

(defn- lsp-drain (state replies)
  "Carry out what a fold of server output (`lsp-ingest` / `lsp-dispatch`) left to do, and
answer the state without it: `replies` to their waiters, anything we owe the SERVER down
its stdin, and anything it asked us to CHANGE (a code action's command, applied by the loop
that owns the buffers). Here rather than in the fold, so the fold stays pure.

What the server SAID — its notices and diagnostics — stays on the state for [[lsp-flush]]:
a reply is someone waiting, a log line is not, and sending each line as it arrived is what
froze the editor while Expert indexed."
  (doseq (r replies) (send (first r) (second r)))
  (lsp-send-all (:proc state) (or (:outbox state) []))
  (doseq (e (or (:edits state) [])) (send :editor [:lsp-apply-edit e]))
  ;; …and any document whose incremental sync it had to refuse: the editor sends the whole
  ;; text next time ([[lsp-sync-document]])
  (doseq (uri (or (:resyncs state) [])) (send :editor [:lsp-resync uri (self)]))
  (lsp-flush-when-due (dissoc state :outbox :edits :resyncs)))

(defn- lsp-said? (state)
  ;; whether a batch of notices or diagnostics is waiting to be sent to the editor
  (or (not (empty? (or (:notices state) []))) (not (empty? (or (:diags state) [])))))

(defn- lsp-flush (state)
  "Send the editor what the server said since the last flush, as ONE `[:lsp-notices …]`
event and one `[:lsp-diagnostics …]` per file ([[lsp-publish]] already kept only the
newest per open file), and note when. The editor's loop pays a turn per event, so a burst
of a thousand log lines costs it one."
  (let (notices (or (:notices state) []))
    (when (not (empty? notices)) (send :editor [:lsp-notices notices]))
    ;; a file's diagnostics only matter at their newest: one this server published that
    ;; the editor has not folded yet is REPLACED rather than queued behind (brood ADR-490).
    ;; The log above is not state — every line of it counts — so it is a plain send.
    (doseq (d (or (:diags state) []))
      (let ([path items raw] d)
        (proc/send-latest :editor
          [:lsp-diagnostics (pr-str (self)) path]
          [:lsp-diagnostics path items raw (self)])))
    (assoc (dissoc state :notices :diags) :flushed-at (os/now))))

(defn- lsp-flush-wait (state)
  ;; ms until the waiting batch is due, or nil when nothing waits
  (when (lsp-said? state)
    (let (left (math/floor (- (+ (or (:flushed-at state) 0) *lsp-flush-ms*) (os/now))))
      (if (< left 0) 0 left))))

(defn- lsp-flush-when-due (state)
  ;; flush a waiting batch once `*lsp-flush-ms*` has passed since the last; else keep it
  (let (wait (lsp-flush-wait state))
    (if (and (not (nil? wait)) (= wait 0)) (lsp-flush state) state)))

(defn- lsp-await-init (state id)
  "Read frames until the `initialize` response (jsonrpc `id`) arrives, returning the
state with the consumed bytes drained. Bounded (`*lsp-init-timeout-ms*`) so a server that
never replies can't hang the connection process forever.

The bound is on the CONNECTION process, never on the editor: this runs in the pid
`lsp-connect` spawned, and every request has its own timeout, so a slow handshake costs a
late first completion rather than a frozen editor."
  (receive
    ([:proc p chunk]
      (let (buf (str (:buf state) chunk)
            [msgs remaining] (jsonrpc/decode buf)
            hit (seq/find msgs (fn (msg) (and (= (:id msg) id) (nil? (:method msg)))))
            ;; everything else that arrived meanwhile is handled as the loop
            ;; would: a server that asks for its configuration or registers a
            ;; capability BEFORE answering initialize is waiting on us, and
            ;; dropping the request left it waiting for good
            st (apply lsp-drain
                 (fold (seq/reject msgs (fn (msg) (= msg hit)))
                   [(assoc state :buf remaining) '()]
                   lsp-dispatch)))
        (if hit
          ;; …keeping what it says it can do. A server that does not offer
          ;; signature help should not be asked for it on every keystroke,
          ;; and the only honest source for that is its own answer here.
          (assoc st :caps (or (map/get-in hit [:result :capabilities]) {}))
          (lsp-await-init st id))))
    ([:proc-err p chunk] (lsp-await-init state id))
    ([:proc-closed p code] state)
    (after *lsp-init-timeout-ms* state)))

(defn- lsp-folder (root)
  "A project `root` (a path) as an LSP `WorkspaceFolder`."
  {:uri (lsp-path->uri root) :name (path/basename root)})

(defn- lsp-loop (state)
  "The connection's main receive loop: server output folds in via `lsp-ingest` (replies
routed to waiters); an `[:ask …]` request is turned into frames and written to the
server; `:shutdown` or the server dying stops it."
  (receive
    ([:proc p chunk] (lsp-loop (apply lsp-drain (lsp-ingest state chunk))))
    ([:proc-err p chunk] (lsp-loop state))
    ;; what the server said last — often why it is exiting — is flushed, not dropped
    ([:proc-closed p code] (do (lsp-flush state) :lsp-stopped))
    ;; Every question, whatever its kind: the kind (an `LspRequest` record) says what goes
    ;; on the wire and how the answer is shaped, so a new request is an impl, not an arm.
    ([:ask kind corr reply-to request] (let ([st2 frames] (lsp-issue state
                                                            corr reply-to kind request))
                                         (lsp-send-all (:proc st2) frames)
                                         (lsp-loop st2)))
    ;; A SYNC with no question attached. Every `:ask` syncs the document as a side effect
    ;; of asking the server something (`lsp-issue`), which is the whole reason diagnostics
    ;; never appeared: a server PUSHES `publishDiagnostics` after a `didChange`, and nothing
    ;; sent one while you typed. The symptom was exact — imenu worked, because asking for
    ;; symbols synced the text on the way past, and then the findings arrived for that one
    ;; version and never again.
    ;; `sync` is the `{:text|:changes …}` map [[lsp-sync-document]] takes
    ([:sync uri lang sync] (let ([st2 frames] (lsp-sync-document state uri lang sync))
                             (lsp-send-all (:proc st2) frames)
                             (lsp-loop (lsp-drain st2 (list)))))
    ([:close uri] (let ([st2 frames] (lsp-close-document state uri))
                    (lsp-send-all (:proc st2) frames)
                    (lsp-loop st2)))
    ;; a project joining or leaving a server that serves several (`lsp-add-folder`)
    ([:folders added removed] (do
                                (lsp-send-all (:proc state)
                                  [(jsonrpc/notification "workspace/didChangeWorkspaceFolders"
                                     {:event {:added (into [] (map added lsp-folder))
                                              :removed (into [] (map removed lsp-folder))}})])
                                (lsp-loop state)))
    ([:shutdown] (do (lsp-flush state) (os/close (:proc state)) :lsp-stopped))
    ;; a batch of what the server said is due ([[lsp-flush]]); with none waiting this only
    ;; wakes the loop once a minute to wait again
    (after (or (lsp-flush-wait state) 60000) (lsp-loop (lsp-flush-when-due state)))))

(defn- lsp-init-params (root-uri)
  {:processId nil
   :rootUri root-uri
   ;; `workspaceFolders` as well as `rootUri`. `rootUri` is deprecated in the protocol and
   ;; a server written against the current spec may read only this one — which looks from
   ;; the outside exactly like a server that initialises cleanly and then answers nothing,
   ;; because it has no workspace to answer about.
   :workspaceFolders [{:uri root-uri :name (path/basename (lsp-uri->path root-uri))}]
   :clientInfo {:name "bedit"}
   ;; `window.workDoneProgress`: a server that may report progress says what it is doing
   ;; in `$/progress` — the channel meant for the reader — rather than only in its log.
   ;; Expert narrates `[proj] Starting engine node` there. Its log (`window/logMessage`)
   ;; is its whole Logger, the indexer's errors included, and goes to `*lsp-log*` only
   ;; ([[lsp-notice-echo?]]) — so progress is how its startup still reaches the reader.
   :capabilities {:window {:workDoneProgress true}
                  :workspace {:workspaceFolders true :configuration true}
                  :textDocument {:completion {:completionItem {:snippetSupport false}}
                                 :documentSymbol {:hierarchicalDocumentSymbolSupport true}}}})

(defn- lsp-run (cmd cmd-args root-uri)
  "The connection process body: spawn the server, do the `initialize`/`initialized`
handshake, then loop. Must call `proc-spawn` itself so it owns the handle (is the
subscriber for the server's `[:proc …]` output).

A spawn that fails ends this process quietly. Callers ask `os/which` first, so the only
way here is a race (the binary went away between the two) or a server that cannot be
executed at all — and in a spawned process a raise is a `[crash]` trace on the user's
screen, which is a worse answer to that than the connection simply not existing. The
model drops a connection whose process is gone (`ed-lsp-live`)."
  ;; Started IN the project root, not in whatever directory the editor happens to have.
  ;; A language server that builds the project reads its own working directory to find it —
  ;; Expert runs `mix`, rust-analyzer runs `cargo` — and `rootUri` in the handshake does not
  ;; set one. Launched from elsewhere, Expert initialised, reported a full
  ;; `completionProvider`, and then answered every request with nothing, because there was
  ;; no project where it was standing.
  (let (p (check-allow :discarded-catch
            (try (os/spawn cmd cmd-args {:cwd (lsp-uri->path root-uri)}) (catch e nil))))
    (when p (lsp-run-connected p root-uri))))

(defn- lsp-run-connected (p root-uri)
  "The handshake and loop, given a live server handle."
  ;; A server can be gone before the first write: a rustup `rust-analyzer` stub for a
  ;; component that is not installed passes `os/which`, starts, and exits at once. The write
  ;; then raises, and this process ended in a crash trace. It ends quietly instead — the
  ;; editor sees the dead connection and says so once (`ed-lsp-autostart`).
  (let (state {:proc p :buf "" :next-id 1 :pending {} :opened {} :version 2})
    (if (not (lsp-write-handshake p (jsonrpc/request 0 "initialize" (lsp-init-params root-uri))))
      :lsp-stopped (let (state2 (lsp-await-init state 0))
        (if (not (lsp-write-handshake p (jsonrpc/notification "initialized" {})))
          :lsp-stopped (do
            ;; tell the editor what this server can do, so a command can say "this server
            ;; does not do that" instead of showing nothing and reading as broken
            (send :editor [:lsp-capabilities (or (:caps state2) {}) (self)])
            (lsp-loop state2)))))))

(defn- lsp-write-handshake (p frame)
  ;; write one handshake frame; false when the server is already gone (the first write can
  ;; land in the pipe's buffer before a dying server exits, so either may be the one to fail)
  (check-allow :discarded-catch (try (do (os/write p frame) true) (catch _ false))))

(defn lsp-connect (cmd cmd-args root-uri)
  "Spawn language server `cmd` (with `cmd-args`, a list/vector of strings) for project
`root-uri` and return the connection process pid. The editor files it under
`:lsp-servers` (`lsp-commands/ed-lsp-attach`), which is what `lsp-server-entry` finds. `(lsp-shutdown conn)` stops it."
  (spawn (lsp-run cmd cmd-args root-uri)))

(defn lsp-capable? (caps feature)
  "Whether a server's advertised `caps` include `feature` (`:signatureHelpProvider`,
`:codeActionProvider`, …). A provider field is `true`, or an options object, or absent —
all three are answers, and only the last means no.

Asked before a request the reader did not make (the idle beat's signature help) and before
telling them a command is unavailable. Never asked to VETO a request they did make: a
server that under-advertises and answers anyway is a thing that happens, and refusing on
its behalf would be worse than letting it say no itself."
  (let (v (get (or caps {}) feature))
    (not (or (nil? v) (= v false)))))

(defn lsp-shutdown (conn)
  "Stop a connection: kill the server and end the connection process."
  (send conn [:shutdown]))

(defn- lsp-complete-collect (corrs deadline acc)
  "Receive the completion answers for `corrs` until each has come or `deadline` passes,
merged into `acc` (`{:entries :incomplete}`); a server that never answered makes the merge
incomplete, so nothing caches it."
  (if (empty? corrs)
    acc
    (let (left (math/max 0 (- deadline (os/now))))
      (receive
        ([:lsp-completion corr answer] (if (includes? corrs corr)
                                         (lsp-complete-collect (seq/reject corrs
                                                                 (fn (c) (= c corr)))
                                           deadline
                                           {:entries (seq/distinct (append (:entries acc)
                                                                     (:entries answer)))
                                            :incomplete (or (:incomplete acc) (:incomplete answer))})
                                         (lsp-complete-collect corrs deadline acc)))
        (after left (assoc acc :incomplete true))))))

(defn lsp-complete-all (asks timeout)
  "Ask each `[conn request]` in `asks` for completions at its `request` (`{:uri :lang :line
:char}`, with `:text` when that server's copy has to be brought up to date on the way), all
at once, blocking up to `timeout` ms in all. Answers the merge `{:entries :incomplete}`
([[lsp-completion-answer]]): entries in server order, each once. Safe to call only on an
explicit trigger, not per keystroke."
  (let (corrs (into []
                (map asks
                  (fn ([conn request])
                    (let (corr (ref))
                      (lsp-ask-async conn (lsp-completion) corr (self) request)
                      corr)))))
    (lsp-complete-collect corrs (+ (os/now) timeout) {:entries '() :incomplete false})))

(defn lsp-close (conn uri)
  "Tell a running server the document `uri` is closed (`textDocument/didClose`)."
  (send conn [:close uri]))

(defn lsp-add-folder (conn root)
  "Tell a running server it serves project `root` too (`workspace/didChangeWorkspaceFolders`)."
  (send conn [:folders [root] []]))

(defn lsp-remove-folder (conn root)
  "Tell a running server it no longer serves project `root`."
  (send conn [:folders [] [root]]))

;; ---- the async requests: fire the request; the reply lands on `reply-to` ----------
;; Returns at once: the conn's tagged, corr-carrying reply (`[:lsp-definition corr locs]`,
;; `[:lsp-hover corr s]`, …) lands on `reply-to`'s mailbox — the editor registers event
;; handlers for those tags and folds by corr against `:lsp-pending`
;; (lsp-commands/ed-ev-lsp-reply), so a lookup never blocks the loop: jump/show/apply
;; happen when the answer ARRIVES. Completion keeps its bounded sync wait (the popup is
;; modal UX — see `lsp-complete-all`).

(defn lsp-sync (conn uri lang sync)
  "Tell the server the document changed, asking nothing in return. `sync` is the
`{:text|:changes :base :token}` map [[lsp-sync-document]] takes —
the editor sends the edits since its last sync when the server accepts them
(`lsp-commands/ed-lsp-push-text`).

This is what makes DIAGNOSTICS work. A server does not answer a question with them; it
pushes `publishDiagnostics` when it learns the text changed. Requests used to sync the
document only on their way to asking something, so before this existed the findings arrived
only for whatever version you happened to invoke a lookup on — and never while typing,
which is the only time they are worth having."
  (send conn [:sync uri lang sync]))

(defn lsp-ask-async (conn kind corr reply-to request)
  "Fire one request of `kind` (an `LspRequest` record — `lsp-definition`, `lsp-hover`, …)
built from `request`: `{:uri :lang :line :char}` for a question about the text at
point (with `:text` only to sync the whole document on the way — see [[lsp-issue]]),
plus `:diags` (code actions) or `:new-name` (rename); `{:command :arguments}` for
`lsp-execute-command`, which is about no document. The kind's reply —
`[:lsp-definition corr locs]`, `[:lsp-workspace-edit corr wsedit]`, … (see its
`lsp-request-shape`) — lands on `reply-to`.

ONE sender rather than one per kind: each used to be a function, a `lsp-loop` arm and a
request builder, three copies of the same plumbing that differed only in the method name
and the params, which are now the kind's own `lsp-request-method` / `lsp-request-params`."
  (send conn [:ask kind corr reply-to request]))

;; ---- the completion source --------------------------------------------------

(defn- lsp-symbol-at-point (buf)
  "`[start prefix line]` — the symbol before point, the completion region, by the same
boundary rule the other sources use (`symbol-prefix-at`), so the merge keeps this source;
and the current line up to point, which a server's edit columns are read against. Read
from that line alone: a symbol never crosses a line, and flattening the buffer to find it
was a measurable part of every Tab on a big file."
  (let (line-start (buffer-point (beginning-of-line buf))
        line (text/slice (buffer-rope buf) line-start (buffer-point buf))
        [start prefix] (symbol-prefix-at line (string/length line)))
    [(+ line-start start) prefix line]))

(defn lsp-path->uri (path)
  "The `file://` URI of `path`, each segment percent-encoded: a server compares URIs as
strings and sends them encoded, so `/my project/a.ex` has to go out as `my%20project`
or its answers name a file this client never opened."
  (if (nil? path)
    nil ; a buffer with no file has no URI; the request carries null and the server says so
    (str "file://" (string/join (map (string/split path "/") url/percent-encode) "/"))))
(sig lsp-path->uri ((or string nil) -> (or string nil)))

(def *lsp-language-ids*
  ;; file extension → LSP languageId. Servers key document handling off this, so it
  ;; must be the id the server expects (e.g. rust-analyzer wants "rust"). Extend live.
  {"blsp" "brood"
   "rs" "rust"
   "rb" "ruby"
   "rake" "ruby"
   "gemspec" "ruby"
   "ex" "elixir"
   "exs" "elixir"
   "heex" "phoenix-heex"
   "py" "python"
   "pyi" "python"
   "ts" "typescript"
   "mts" "typescript"
   "cts" "typescript"
   "tsx" "typescriptreact"
   "js" "javascript"
   "mjs" "javascript"
   "cjs" "javascript"
   "jsx" "javascriptreact"
   "go" "go"
   "c" "c"
   "h" "c"
   "cc" "cpp"
   "cpp" "cpp"
   "cxx" "cpp"
   "hh" "cpp"
   "hpp" "cpp"
   "hxx" "cpp"
   ;; both servers accept HashiCorp's ids; `-vars` is a variables file
   "tf" "terraform"
   "tofu" "terraform"
   "tfvars" "terraform-vars"
   "cs" "csharp"
   "csx" "csharp"
   "htm" "html"})

(defn lsp-extension (path)
  "The file extension of `path` (without the dot), or \"\" if none."
  (let (dot (string/last-index-of path "."))
    (if (< dot 0) "" (string/substring path (+ dot 1) (string/length path)))))

(defn lsp-language-id (path)
  "The LSP languageId for `path`, from its extension (`*lsp-language-ids*`); the bare
extension when unmapped, or \"\" when there's none."
  (if (nil? path)
    ""
    (let (ext (lsp-extension path))
      (get *lsp-language-ids* ext ext))))

(defn lsp-specs-for (path)
  "The server specs (`*lsp-server-specs*`) that serve `path`'s extension, main servers
first and add-ons after, each group in table order; `[]` for a file no server serves, or
no file: a `.py` file has `python` and the `ruff` add-on."
  (if (not (string? path))
    []
    (let (ext (lsp-extension path)
          serving (seq/filter *lsp-server-specs* (fn (spec) (includes? (:extensions spec) ext))))
      (into []
        (append (seq/reject serving (fn (spec) (:add-on spec)))
          (seq/filter serving (fn (spec) (:add-on spec))))))))

(defn lsp-spec-named (name)
  "The server spec called `name`, or nil."
  (seq/find *lsp-server-specs* (fn (spec) (= (:name spec) name))))

;; ---- which running servers a file belongs to ---------------------------------
;; The model keeps every server it started, `:lsp-servers` — `{conn entry}`, an entry
;; `{:spec name :roots [root …] :conn pid :caps map :identity … :command}` filed under its
;; connection: one process, one entry, whatever projects it comes to serve. A server that
;; serves several projects (`:shared`) has several roots. A file belongs to every entry whose spec serves its extension and one of
;; whose roots holds it.
;;
;; There used to be ONE connection, `:lsp`, and every buffer with a file was pushed to it:
;; open a `.blsp` file and brood-lsp started, then open wos kernel source and brood-lsp
;; checked THAT as Brood (`unbound symbol: var` on every line), while a Rust file beside
;; them never got rust-analyzer at all.

(defn lsp-under? (file root)
  "True when `file` is `root` or inside it — a directory, not a prefix: `/p/crate` does not
hold `/p/crate2/x`. False for a buffer with no file."
  (and (string? file)
    (or (= file root)
      (string/starts-with? file (if (string/ends-with? root "/") root (str root "/"))))))

(defn lsp-entry-root (entry file)
  "The deepest of `entry`'s roots that holds `file`, or nil."
  (last (seq/sort-by (seq/filter (:roots entry) (fn (root) (lsp-under? file root))) string/length)))

(defn lsp-known-root (m spec-name file)
  "The project root server `spec-name` was found to start in for `file`'s folder
(`:lsp-roots`, filled by `lsp-commands/ed-lsp-root-for`), or nil while nobody has looked."
  (get (or (:lsp-roots m) {}) [spec-name (path/dirname file)]))

(defn lsp-server-entries (m file)
  "Every running server on model `m` that serves `file`, as entries carrying their `:key`
(the connection they are filed under),
main server first (`lsp-specs-for`'s order): per spec, the entry serving the file's own
project root when it is known (`lsp-known-root`), else the one whose root holding the file
is deepest. A nested project inside a running server's root is not that server's: until
its own starts it has none, which is what lets it start. Asks no disk and no process, so it
is cheap enough for every frame; whether a connection is still alive is `lsp-live-entries`'
question."
  (let (pairs (%map-pairs (or (:lsp-servers m) {})))
    (into []
      (seq/keep (lsp-specs-for file)
        (fn (spec)
          (let (known (lsp-known-root m (:name spec) file)
                hits (seq/filter pairs
                       (fn ([key entry])
                         (and (= (:spec entry) (:name spec))
                           (if known
                             (includes? (:roots entry) known)
                             (lsp-entry-root entry file))))))
            (when (not (empty? hits))
              (let ([key entry] (last (seq/sort-by hits
                                        (fn ([_ entry]) (string/length (lsp-entry-root entry file))))))
                (assoc entry :key key)))))))))

(defn lsp-server-entry (m file)
  "The main running server for `file` (the first of `lsp-server-entries`), or nil."
  (first (lsp-server-entries m file)))

(defn lsp-live-entries (m file)
  "`lsp-server-entries` whose process is still alive — a pid nothing answers costs every
request its timeout."
  (into [] (seq/filter (lsp-server-entries m file) (fn (entry) (proc/alive? (:conn entry))))))

(defn lsp-conn (m file)
  "The main live connection that serves `file`, or nil."
  (:conn (first (lsp-live-entries m file))))

(defn lsp-entry-for (m file feature)
  "The live server to ask about `file` for `feature` (`:hoverProvider`, …): the first that
says it offers it, else the main one, which may answer anyway or say no itself; nil with
none alive."
  (let (live (lsp-live-entries m file))
    (or (seq/find live (fn (entry) (lsp-capable? (:caps entry) feature))) (first live))))

(defn lsp-folders-supported? (caps)
  "Whether a server's `caps` say it takes projects added and removed while it runs
(`workspace.workspaceFolders` supported, with change notifications) — the condition for
serving several from one process."
  (let (folders (map/get-in (or caps {}) [:workspace :workspaceFolders] {}))
    (and (= true (:supported folders))
      (let (notify (:changeNotifications folders))
        (or (= notify true) (string? notify))))))

(defn- lsp-complete-request (m entry file buf)
  "The completion request for `entry`'s server: with the text only when that server's copy
is not this buffer's (completion's prepare step normally synced it just before)."
  (let (uri (lsp-path->uri file)
        synced (map/get-in m [:lsp-synced [(:key entry) uri] :buf])
        request {:uri uri
                 :lang (lsp-language-id file)
                 :line (buffer-current-line buf)
                 :char (buffer-column buf)})
    (if (and synced (= (buffer-rope synced) (buffer-rope buf)))
      request
      (assoc request :text (buffer-text buf)))))

(defn lsp-complete-source (m)
  "The LSP completion source (a `complete-at-point/*completion-sources*` entry): `[start prefix
candidates opts]` from the buffer's servers that offer completion (the main one when none says),
asked at once and merged; `nil` when none serves its file, the buffer has no file, or every
server timed out. `complete` merges these with the mode and buffer-word sources.

`opts` is `{:cache true}` unless a server said its list was incomplete: the popup then
narrows by filtering these candidates as you type, and asks the servers again only when the
prefix stops extending this one (`complete-at-point/complete-at`). An EMPTY complete answer
is an answer too — nothing for `foo` means nothing for `foob` — so it is returned, not nil,
or every narrowing key would ask again.

A connection whose process has died counts as off: this source runs on every Tab, and a
pid nothing answers would spend `*lsp-timeout-ms*` per press waiting for a reply that
cannot come."
  (let (buf (ed-current-buf m)
        file (buffer-file buf)
        live (lsp-live-entries m file)
        offering (seq/filter live (fn (entry) (lsp-capable? (:caps entry) :completionProvider)))
        asked (cond (not (empty? offering)) offering (empty? live) [] else [(first live)]))
    (if (or (empty? asked) (nil? file))
      nil
      (let ([start prefix line] (lsp-symbol-at-point buf)
            answer (lsp-complete-all (map asked
                                       (fn (entry)
                                         [(:conn entry) (lsp-complete-request m entry file buf)]))
                     *lsp-timeout-ms*)
            separators (complete-at-point/completion-separators m)
            word-start (- (string/length line) (string/length prefix))
            candidates (seq/distinct (seq/keep (:entries answer)
                                       (fn (entry)
                                         (lsp-completion-candidate line word-start entry separators)))))
        (cond
          (not (:incomplete answer)) [start prefix candidates {:cache true}]
          (empty? candidates) nil
          :else [start prefix candidates])))))
